WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Shinjin the ruler, all the power of Shinjin is in your hands.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:28.640
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.000
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.160
Can you really have all the power of Shinjin in your hands?

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.060
Like, Shinjin is not...

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:38.020
Shinjin is a person, right?

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:50.040
You wouldn't say all the powers of, you know, Mahatma Gandhi in your hands, right, if you were playing some kind of, like, 1945 Indian independence adventure.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:56.480
I would associate it more with, like, a sports game, like, all the power of LeBron James in your hand.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:08.460
Not knowing that this was marketing for the states, a, you know, a foreign historic ruler that I wouldn't have learned about in high school.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.180
It's a weird, it's a weird flex.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.220
Yep. All that stuff you never knew in the palm of your hands.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.080
It's the internet. But it's Shinjin the ruler on NES in 1990.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:25.320
And let me tell you, the strategy game video game universe just got bigger.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:34.220
Because Takeda Shinjin is basically, you know, at this point, I mean, he is somewhat related to Nobunaga.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.100
Yeah, he's like another guy on the map, right?

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.660
Yeah, he's another guy on the map.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:39.860
Now he gets a game.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:49.300
But Nobunaga and Shinjin are bonded through the marriage of Nobunaga's daughter to Shinjin's son.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.120
So this is, like, it's all connected, man.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.860
So you did some actual research here.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.900
Wait till you see what I hit you up with later in the episode.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.540
That's called a tease in the marketing business.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.280
But, yeah, there's some stuff going on in this game.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:11.000
But, yeah, I did a little bit of research because Shinjin, the ruler, takes place around the same time as Nobunaga.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:11.960
So I'm like, wait a minute.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.820
Who the heck actually unified Japan?

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.420
Was it not Nobunaga?

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:24.400
It's just one of those things, Sean, where, you know, like the textbook tells you something, but history says it's a lot more complicated.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:33.200
And, you know, depending on which way you read it, it's Nobunaga, it's Shinjin, or, and I'm not going to try to pronounce this, it's a third guy.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.880
So it was, like, three people.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.880
Obviously, no one man has the power to just unify an entire country, right?

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:45.620
Like, that's like saying George Washington won the battle against Britain, right?

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:49.540
Like, he didn't single-handedly just turn, I mean, you know.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:56.300
Aren't there, like, mangas out there or animes that sort of, like, would do that?

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:02.700
Like, there's, like, an anime version of George Washington who single-handedly defeated the British with, like, a spirit bomb or something?

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:09.780
There was that steampunk game on 3DS where, like, George Washington and Abraham Lincoln take on a bunch of mechs.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.420
But that's not what you're talking about.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.020
No, no, I don't think I'm talking about anything.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:23.620
Yeah. So, with Shinjin the ruler, I have to ask you the question that's on everybody's mind.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.780
It's the first question that they ask you in the game.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.700
And that's, how many occupied regions do you control, Sean?

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.560
This was, like, a really funny moment.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.920
How many occupied regions do you control?

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.980
Is it 0, 3, 2, or 1?

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.620
First off, why are they in that order?

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.000
Yes. But also, is this covered in the manual?

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.200
Okay, so I did read the manual.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.800
The manual needs to be used in this game.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:03.560
But because everything is written down as, like, some super abridged version of what you're doing.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.680
And there's no, like, flavor text or tool tips to help you out.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.520
So, it's very on brand for this game to just immediately be like,

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.820
So, you already know what's going on.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.120
How would you like to proceed?

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.720
So, I started by saying three.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:20.940
And they're like, silly, Sean.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.700
You don't have that many.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:25.900
And I'm like, okay, let me try again with two.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.680
And they said the same thing.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.180
And then, finally, I went with one.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.360
And so, I, like, you know more about this than me.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.480
Because you're the host.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:45.840
But is that just, like, how many territories aside from your capital you'd also like to give, like, orders to?

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:47.320
Well, you are spot on.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.620
So, kudos to that.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:49.960
That is true.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:54.040
But it is funny that the game can never start with more than one.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.120
So, it is not a choice at the beginning.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.920
It's like a pop quiz at first.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:01.260
Like, how many occupied regions do you control?

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.020
And the answer is only one.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.100
It can never be two at the start of a game.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:11.080
You only ever start with the one region that Jinjin did have rule over.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.120
And then, even if you go beyond three.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.500
Say you own, you know, four or five territories.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:21.840
You can only ever give commands to three at a time.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:23.960
Which I think is, that makes sense.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.020
It's like a balance thing.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.120
Yes, exactly.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.020
Right. You can't just be ganging up.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.740
You know, if you have 17 and there's two remaining, right?

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:36.020
It's like, all right. Just, at this point, guys, just give in.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.240
But, yes.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.340
It's how many you want to give orders to.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:41.960
So, I get the check later on.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:48.020
I just think it's funny as a starting point to basically say, how many occupied regions do you control?

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.540
Is just a weird way of wording.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.000
How many territories do you want to give orders to?

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:55.760
Is the real question at hand.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.960
Which is also, like, just thinking about strategy games now.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.900
Like, it's already dating itself.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:08.480
Like, it would just be, here's what you can do.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.800
You can choose to do this or skip.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.140
Not, how many of the things that you are able to do would you like to do?

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:17.660
It just doesn't happen.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:18.920
Yeah, that's a good point.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.260
Because you can pass.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.520
You can say, like, I choose to do nothing.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.720
Which, I guess, is also awful.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.900
Because, technically, I think you can give more.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.020
Depending on what you choose, you can give more than one command to a territory.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:34.440
So, you might want to just give off, like, one command and then pass the remainder.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.400
But, I could never find a good reason for that.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.540
You need to do everything in this game.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.720
So, you might as well never pass.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.960
I can't think of a justification for it.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.300
But, I wanted to talk about the other thing you brought up.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:49.840
Which is just the confusing menu layout.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:52.420
Because everything is abbreviated.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:55.100
And, when I say everything, I mean it all.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.600
The manual is required because memorization would be required otherwise.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.620
You have your YLD, your DST, your PRD, your PRI.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.600
And, then, within those menus, your RNK and your HDQ.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.360
It's like, oh, come on.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.100
Give me a break here. It's not even like, oh, how are they supposed to list all this out?

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:15.920
It's like, no.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:20.200
The way that they designed, like, the UX UI developer.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.180
Whoever decided that this is how this game is going to look.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:29.640
Like, they made the conscious decision to have to abbreviate everything.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:30.940
Like, the...

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:33.920
The... Okay.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:35.060
So, each, like, yeah.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.640
The Yield. The Destructive Capacity.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:43.160
Like, they're all in huge, like, 25-point font.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.300
Or 32-point font.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:50.000
And, it could have just as easily been, like, the standard...

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.980
I don't even know what the typeface is.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.420
But, that typical Nintendo game typeface.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.160
It could have been that. They could have written everything out.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:03.540
And, they could have given you a bit more context with what you were doing.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:05.580
And, it all would have worked.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:11.640
It just wouldn't have been, like, this sort of badge square design that they're going for here.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:16.800
Yeah. In the combat sequences, you do get a lot of information in those menus.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:17.780
A lot more text.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:19.100
So, I agree with you.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:21.700
It's not hidden for any good reason.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.300
But, even just thinking about, you know.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.160
Okay. So, we've played strategy games before.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:34.100
But, that doesn't mean that everybody in 1990 played all of the other Koei games.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:35.760
Of which, this is not a Koei game, too.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:36.880
We'll get into that later.

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:38.100
But, you know.

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:39.580
We have some introduction to this.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:45.480
So, we kind of at least understand the importance of rice and building a military and taxes.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:48.340
Right? Because, we've seen how it works in the other games.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:51.240
And, there's generally a flow of these things.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:58.380
But, now the fact that I have to actually refer back to the manual just to understand, like, what the abbreviations mean.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.280
Unless I memorize it all.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:13.040
It just feels like another way to kind of gatekeep this strategy genre away from casuals who would prefer to just, like, I don't know, have a streamlined version of this.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:47.280
And, I get that that's just sort of the time that we're in.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:48.720
Like, every game.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:49.920
Even if it's an action game.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:51.540
Like, or an RPG.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:58.060
Like, it's assuming that you also have the instruction booklet or the manual out.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.720
And, they both kind of work in tandem.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.900
But, when it gets, like, when it gets this amorphous.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:12.860
When it gets, uh, this much needs to be referenced to in the book.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.340
Like, what is, this isn't the game anymore.

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:22.540
This is just a, like, an engine for a pen and paper thing that you're doing off to the side.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:25.340
I, I've, I'm exaggerating a bit here.

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:39.100
But, that's sort of what it felt like with how little, like, this, this game, aside from the battles that we'll get into, almost just feels like a calculator for a game that you're playing in the, in the instruction booklet.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:41.000
Yep. Mm-hmm.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.560
And, uh, Hot B is, it feels funny.

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:46.680
Hot Boy. Yeah, Hot B. I just kept saying Hot Boy.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.540
Yeah, Hot Boy, uh, is making this game.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.000
Uh, they're basically making a co-A game, though, right?

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.860
Like, you could, you could say that, yes, the presentation is different.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:02.860
But, uh, what your, what your main goal is, is not too different from Nobunaga's ambition.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:10.100
And, even the kinds of things that, uh, you should focus on, or, um, the military battles, right?

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.560
That, they're, they're still turn-based.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:16.480
Granted, they're a little more, like, Fire Emblem here than they were in Nobunaga's.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:18.940
Uh, you know, what is it?

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:23.120
Does it feel different, or does it feel too close to those co-A games?

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:28.380
Is it a bootleg, or is it an entirely different approach to the Nobunaga genre?

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:38.260
No, I think that there's enough going on in the battles, uh, to differentiate between this and, like, Nobunaga and romance.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:45.100
Um, where, you're right, like, I, I didn't actually put this together, but it does feel a lot more like a Fire Emblem.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:53.240
And I think that, uh, everything about this game compared to those is, like, it just looks cleaner.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:12:03.220
Like, even the ridiculous design of the domestic, uh, the domestic orders and what you're doing outside of battle.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:07.120
Like, it, it's, it's nonsense, but it looks nicer.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:21.960
And in the battles, like, in, in Nobunaga, like, you had these weird, like, almost skeuomorphic, like, like, pieces that you were moving around on this weird grid that wasn't a hex grid, but it tried to act like one.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:27.540
And it all looked very, uh, chunky and not very clean.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:45.220
Whereas the battles in this game, like, everything feels a lot more like a, a 8-bit strategy game than, like, what Nobunaga seemed to have been going for, which is almost like trying to pretend that it's a board game.

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:49.980
So what's your fast approach to learning these games now that we've had a few, right?

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:54.300
Uh, it's not, it's not your first time kind of digesting what's going on here.

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:58.500
Did, did you, did you go straight to the manual and kind of read through everything?

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:01.800
Did you try to just say, like, oh, it's all about production.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:03.460
I gotta just up my production.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:04.920
Is it all about military?

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:08.840
I've gotta just, uh, acquire as many troops as possible.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:13.400
What was your strategy, uh, to kind of trying to get somewhere with this game?

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:17.520
I guess I'll start by saying that I don't think that I truly understand these games still.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:26.080
Um, with all of the abstract, uh, stuff going on, it just doesn't hook me as much.

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:35.940
So it's hard. It's like, even from the beginning, from Nobunaga to today, I still don't think I have a full grasp of how all this stuff works.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:43.040
But, I will say that I was like, okay, yeah, I do kind of understand this a bit more than the first one that we played.

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:48.860
So I'll try to just go in, and then when I have to reference something, I'll go to the manual and I'll reference it.

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:54.720
And then I realized that, yeah, everything is just like a letter, and you have to know what that letter means.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:14:10.420
So I did then just have to go and kind of read the manual as if I was doing a unit for, like, I had to read the textbook to do a pop quiz on the test in high school.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:19.400
So, it was a lot of front-loading of information, uh, which I don't think is very conducive to actually learning how to play the game.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:25.760
But I did my best, and I, you know, I didn't crash out in the first year like I've done in previous games.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:31.780
And I did win a couple battles, and I still don't know, like, if I'm doing well.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:37.260
But, uh, yeah, I didn't immediately lose.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:43.420
Right, and I didn't beat the game either, or whatever that would entail, conquering all the territories.

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:51.920
But I would say, similar to your point about how, like, okay, now that we've played a few of these, I know at least a little bit more than I did when we went in blind.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:59.640
There are a lot of menus here that I, this is not a strategy guide, this is not game FAQs, don't listen to me.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:07.540
But for me, there are a lot of things that I just choose to ignore as, like, oh, well, that's just, that's just affecting, like, my chance rolls.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:13.760
So, uh, some of the abbreviated things, like DST for disaster, CLT for culture.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:17.120
These are just, like, yeah, if my culture was higher, it'd be better.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.800
But I'm not focusing on that because that won't help me gain territories.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:24.860
Like, it's better for me, uh, probably in the long run.

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:28.160
But I don't have time to do everything in a turn.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:38.380
So I basically focused on, like, my yield to make sure that I was able to, uh, you know, uh, have enough crop for everybody to not starve.

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:39.880
And then my military.

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:43.020
So it's, you know, it's basically what it comes down to in real life, right?

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:44.260
Use your brain, guys.

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:45.860
Art's not going to get you anywhere.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:48.140
Nobody cares how cultured you are.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:52.780
It's all about how strong your military is and how much money you have.

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:55.140
Loyalty? Who cares about loyalty?

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:02.440
Okay, so I was under the impression that you would actually have, like, uprisings if you didn't keep your loyalty up.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:08.520
So I was trying to at least keep a little bit of, uh, of my resources there.

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:15.520
And I think that culture is, like, an almost universal, like, modifier on how productive you are.

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:18.200
Um, I could be wrong.

00:16:18.200 --> 00:16:19.420
No, you're right about both.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.160
Loyalty will cause an uprising if it's too low, right?

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:25.600
But it's like, all right, I'll, you know, I'll deal with that as we go.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.940
And doing a lot of things will really reduce your loyalty.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:30.960
So it's, I don't know.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:35.360
I can't say whether it's a good or a bad thing to focus on that.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:40.280
But I agree with you because the one time that I was like, oh, you know what?

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:44.220
This is something that hasn't been in these other games that we've played.

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:47.900
Like, you can, like, sort of invest in gold mining.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:51.200
And this was just my experience.

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:52.600
I'll do that.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:53.920
And, like, oh, we got a gold mine.

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:56.920
And then, like, four turns later, it's like, oh, it collapsed.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:00.680
And I'm like, okay, I don't even know if I got anything out of that.

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:10.820
So anytime I tried to make progress in a lot of things, it would just sort of revert a little bit while longer.

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:12.940
So I don't know, man.

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:14.680
You know better than me.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:21.120
No, it's an interesting case with the gold mines, too, because there's a choice within there of, like, oh, you select a gold mine.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.300
Do you want to dig or do you want to produce?

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.160
And it's like, what's the difference?

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:27.020
Well, you can only produce if you have a gold mine.

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:30.180
Yeah, yeah. So I'm like, is this a trick question?

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:32.780
Like, I want more gold mines, right?

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:36.520
Like, the goal is money here, folks.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.440
So I just kept saying, dig gold mines.

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:46.460
And then every time I had a chance to recruit more units, I was recruiting more units because that's the name of the game here.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:54.420
All right, I'm sure there's a way where I can, like, send a princess and have some kind of amicable relationship with the territory to kind of, like, down the line.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:55.800
I'm not sure how you get princesses, either.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:59.360
Yeah. I think they just appear, just like the heirs, though, too, right?

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:00.280
Like, it takes time.

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:01.980
There's a section for heirs.

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:05.400
And it's funny because it's not A-I-R, like air.

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:09.820
It's heirs, like people who can assume the throne.

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:10.820
Inherit your stuff. Yeah.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:13.980
Right. And at the start of the game, it just says no.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:16.660
Air? No.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:18.200
You don't have an air.

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:20.140
Eventually you will.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:24.080
But, like, those are other ways to, I feel like, conquer territories.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:29.020
But like any good video gamer, I can just do it through combat.

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:36.920
So I'm going to conquer all these territories by sending my large military to somebody's smaller military and fighting them in combat.

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:49.360
And I would say that Shinjin the Ruler figured out combat in a way where the Koei games chose to probably just take a more, like, strategical, simulated approach.

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:57.400
And Shinjin the Ruler said, like, all right, this is our chance to lean into video games a little more and make it where you have.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:18:59.960
And these aren't the actual terms, Christian.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:01.160
But you have, like, archers.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.580
You have, you know, you have your.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:06.700
There's a little bit of rock, paper, scissors going on.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:11.020
Yeah, exactly. There is a, there's that, again, Fire Emblem triangle thing.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:14.560
Yeah. But, you know, it's actually, it's gamified.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:20.500
And there is a way to just, you know, take advantage of the computer AI who isn't quite as smart.

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:30.220
Whereas in, to go back to Nobunaga, right, it was like tiles where you had to just kind of assign units and everything was very close together.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:33.900
And it felt more board game-y than it did video game.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:38.980
And Shinjin the Ruler kind of corrects that and made it a little easier to conquer a territory.

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:45.560
So, yes, listener, I did conquer some territories in this game before I completely ran out of product.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:47.240
But you still have to move.

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:51.580
And moving doesn't necessarily mean what you think it means.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:54.140
You kind of have to have, like, internal supply lines, right?

00:19:54.140 --> 00:20:00.420
And, like, it's still way more complex than I would have liked it to be.

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:10.320
Which, I guess, again, that's my modern take on something that would be, I guess, a given in this level of strategy game back in the day.

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:22.740
As you continue to play the game, time moves on and you will have an heir.

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:31.740
And eventually there is a form of succession in this game where, you know, you're hoping that the educational level of your heir was good enough.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:38.960
So that when now they're the ruler and you control them, you kind of have the same or maybe even better loyalty, culture, etc.

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:41.540
But that's a funny mechanic.

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:51.820
Because Shinjin's son, in real life, screwed up things so bad after his father's death that he lost the Empire of Japan in a single day.

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:56.000
So at least it's funny that they, like, built that into the game.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:01.080
But just try not to let that happen with your first heir in this game.

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:07.500
Okay, so this is more of an alternate history game than it is a history simulator.

00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:16.580
Okay. Should they have made it where it's like a race against time to make sure that you unify Japan before your idiot son comes to power?

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:18.640
I think that...

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:30.080
Okay. What I think is funny about this game is that it has these mechanics like heirs, like marrying off your daughters.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:33.720
Like, all these things that in...

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:40.740
I'm used to them being in, like, something like, again, like Crusader Kings where it's all systematized.

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:43.140
Like, you just get a random...

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:45.720
Like, you'll find some character on the map.

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:46.560
You'll marry them.

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:48.640
There's, like, a dice roll every month.

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:50.260
You might have an heir.

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:55.860
And it depends on how you play that these things will happen.

00:21:55.880 --> 00:22:04.160
And in this game, you just, like, I guess on, like, turn 32, then you have an heir that you have to, like, train.

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:13.540
I mean, it's a weird combination where the mechanic feels like it should be more systemic, but it's actually very scripted.

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:15.420
I don't know if that makes any sense.

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:18.780
But I thought it was weird.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:36.820
Another thing that's weird when you think about just the storylines of history and stuff like that, we haven't gotten a game yet, whether it's Shinjin or Nobunaga or Genghis Khan, we haven't gotten a game that really has an accurate portrayal of that person's leadership.

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:49.100
It's always just you as the player assuming a military or a government command post with no knowledge, expertise, or anything like what they had.

00:22:49.260 --> 00:23:00.160
You know, in the Civilization games by Sid Meier later on, there are, like, unique specific bonuses that you get per country for choosing, like, that country's great leader.

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:03.320
And it plays well to their historical beat.

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:10.040
But in Genghis Khan, like, you're not playing as Genghis Khan and all of the, like, stats he had.

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:15.240
You're just playing as, like, the every guy who was just trying to redo what Genghis Khan did.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:17.380
But don't you have stats, though?

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:30.120
Like, that's their equivalent is just I have a higher number in leadership, so I'll be more effective in this specific part of battle.

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:40.920
Where it's not so explicit as saying, well, I'm Theodore Roosevelt, which means that I get a B-52 when I get to that certain tech level.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:47.680
It's more, it's less interesting.

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:51.540
But I still see that they're trying to differentiate in some way.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:54.440
I don't know if that's sort of what you're talking about, though.

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:57.560
No, I think it's a good, it's a fair, it's a fair point back to me.

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:03.180
I guess I just didn't feel like, you know, you know how certain video games make you feel like you're Batman.

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:05.840
Oh, yeah. This does not make you feel like that.

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:29.360
I don't know anything about what it means to feel like Shenzhen in the first place, but I definitely just felt like it was me, the idiot in 2024, trying to figure out what was going on, instead of the cold and calculated Shenzhen who managed to negotiate affairs and build up his culture and take on other warriors in order to unite Japan.

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:34.360
It doesn't feel as brilliant as it was in history.

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:36.120
It feels more like dumb luck.

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:47.200
Because even at the mechanical level, and we've talked about this in these previous strategy games that we played, but how am I supposed to know if that stat is good or not?

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:55.560
I know that it's usually going to be on a scale from 0 to 100 or from 0 to 255 or something, but is it...

00:24:56.660 --> 00:25:04.380
Like, okay, I spent this amount of money on culture to get that stat up.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:09.340
And it says, like, okay, you spent that amount of money, and now your culture stat is 6 higher.

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:11.840
I'm like, okay, is that, like, efficient?

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:13.640
Is that a good return on my investment?

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:17.700
Like, I don't know unless I put 40 hours into this game.

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:22.980
Right. And I don't think that that's, like, really what I would need to do to get an idea.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:28.280
If Hot B really wanted to take on Koei in this genre of games...

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:29.780
Oh, Hot Boy, sorry.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:40.600
Then what they should have done is they should have built in, like, some kind of tutorialization or some kind of guru that you can consult with on things.

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:49.460
Because this is just another entry in these grand strategy NES games that proves that it's very necessary to have tutorials.

00:25:49.460 --> 00:26:00.900
And it bothers me that there is a very arbitrary sequence of inputs that you can do on controllers 1 and 2 in order to get a sound test to pop up.

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:06.240
So they had no problem making it so this way it's like, oh, yeah, let's build a sound test into the game, right?

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:08.220
But let's not have tutorials.

00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:12.800
Don't do all the, like, you know, extra things you think you have to do.

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:17.840
And not even sound test, but there are similarly complicated cutscene codes.

00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:20.560
I didn't know anything about this stuff.

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:22.380
Yeah, it's like, all right, listen to this.

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:24.380
So hold start and select...

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:27.320
Oh, sorry, hold A and B on controller 1.

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:29.700
Start and select was if you're on the Famicom.

00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:32.080
Hold A and B on controller 1.

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:33.360
Set the game.

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:39.980
And as soon as it boots up, release the buttons and hold A and B and right on controller 2.

00:26:40.340 --> 00:26:43.080
If done correctly, the sound test will appear.

00:26:43.340 --> 00:26:45.140
Get the heck out of here.

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:47.420
Like, build a tutorial, hot boy.

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:56.820
I mean, the only credit that I can give them here is that maybe this was not supposed to be a thing.

00:26:57.320 --> 00:27:02.740
And some developer on their team just left this in.

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:04.460
It was how they tested sound.

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:12.040
And it wasn't like, oh, they'll appreciate this sound test because there's such great sound in this game.

00:27:12.180 --> 00:27:13.860
I think it's just left-hors.

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:18.860
Exactly, yeah, it was a testing thing because, yeah, there aren't such great sounds in this game.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:21.540
You'll hear them through the episode.

00:27:23.500 --> 00:27:30.520
Another thing that's kind of lost to history here is that this game takes place during the Sengoku period in Japan.

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:36.440
And, you know, women did have a strategic role, at least behind the scenes, right?

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:40.060
You couldn't... There was no women leaders, but they did...

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:43.940
And there are a lot of notable figures that aren't really, like...

00:27:43.940 --> 00:27:48.920
I don't know. They have the princess mechanic to, like, marry off to kind of create cultural bonds.

00:27:49.120 --> 00:27:50.920
But there was, like, an entire...

00:27:50.920 --> 00:28:03.380
This girl here, Mochizuki Kiyomei, had an entire network of female spies that basically, like, helped Shinjin, like, take down rivals and stuff like that.

00:28:03.380 --> 00:28:12.040
And it's, you know, it's telling of video games of the time that there isn't really a role for women here in this video game.

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:16.080
But I think it's worth mentioning today that they definitely did play a role.

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:20.720
And so their absence here feels noted now less than it probably did in 1990.

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:26.380
I guess, like, I just don't know anything about Shinjin.

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:32.320
So I wouldn't know that, but that is cool to know.

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:38.540
But I guess if I had to, like, find a way, like, oh, you know, they got that in there somehow.

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:39.760
It's ninjas.

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:51.720
Ninjas. Which, as a mechanic, I think it's just funny to, like, to talk about ninjas in a historical setting because they just sound so cartoonish.

00:28:52.040 --> 00:29:00.960
But in this game, they would be how you spy on people or how you, you know, commit terrorist attacks to try and throw your enemies off their game.

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:05.400
And it's just weird to think of ninjas and not think of cartoons.

00:29:06.920 --> 00:29:15.020
But I guess they also are a stand-in for, you know, Shinjin's women's corps.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:20.320
And the ninjas are really, like, what, a way to see the stats of the other territories?

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:29.700
Yeah, it's just the way that you check other stats or, you know, as some kind of clandestine operation.

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:42.780
On the sequels and spinoff sides, it's worth noting that this is actually Shinjin 2.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:46.800
We never got Shinjin 1 in the States.

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:52.060
So I guess we missed out on a lot of the deep lore, and that's why Sean doesn't really understand who Shinjin was.

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:54.940
It wasn't because we didn't read about him in social studies.

00:29:54.940 --> 00:29:57.800
It's because you never got to play the first game.

00:29:57.920 --> 00:29:59.540
Right, Sean? Yeah.

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:00.880
Yeah, that must be it.

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:06.220
I think the first game, actually, though, it's not an origin story for Shinjin.

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:09.820
It's just a more bare-bones version of this same exact game.

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:14.260
And so they had the courtesy to wait until they improved it to send it over here.

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:17.160
Still weird that they would send it over here at all, though, right?

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:20.620
Yeah. Like, did Nobunaga sell well?

00:30:20.620 --> 00:30:22.540
And they were like, oh, then our game can go over there.

00:30:23.240 --> 00:30:25.940
That's definitely something that I kind of wanted to mention.

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:34.860
This is such a weird title to bring over here because, like, we don't learn about this stuff.

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:37.120
Like, I mean, and I...

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:40.540
There are so many other games that are more...

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:41.400
Not appropriate.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:44.440
I'm not saying that this is inappropriate for American audiences.

00:30:44.720 --> 00:30:49.800
But make more sense to try and market to an American audience that didn't get sent over here.

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:55.260
And now you've got this, like, super in-depth Japanese history simulator.

00:30:55.260 --> 00:30:57.000
They're like, oh, no, they'll love this.

00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:00.540
All right. So here's a real, like, spinoff.

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:03.480
Nobunaga's Ambition, Lord of Darkness.

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:05.600
It's worth mentioning that, obviously...

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:22.320
Lord of Darkness. And even though it's not a direct spinoff of Shinjin the Ruler, it's worth mentioning because it is, like, the next version of Nobunaga's Ambition, which is obviously around the same period and everything, souped up for the Super Nintendo.

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:25.140
Kind of weird to think that that becomes, like, a franchise.

00:31:25.900 --> 00:31:27.140
Lord of Darkness?

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:34.420
Like, what if there was a George Washington Revolutionary War simulator and it was just, like, Symphony of the Night?

00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:36.400
Yep, yep.

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:40.640
But the Lord of Darkness isn't, like, some kind of small subtitle either.

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:44.480
It's, like, it's very big on the box.

00:31:44.480 --> 00:31:50.120
It's, like, a very strong-looking Nobunaga, and then it's just Lord of Darkness over him.

00:31:50.240 --> 00:31:54.140
Is it a vampire, like, remix of Japanese history?

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:55.900
Doesn't appear to be.

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:57.820
That would be pretty fucking awesome, though.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:04.500
Nobunaga's Ambition, though, also just came to the Super Nintendo as Nobunaga's Ambition.

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:07.980
So this is, like, a sequel to those games.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:10.100
Yeah. Wow. Yeah.

00:32:10.260 --> 00:32:16.200
It's a complicated release thing because you have Nobunaga's Ambition, Nobunaga's Ambition 2,

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:26.440
Nobunaga's Ambition Lord of Darkness, then a bunch of Japanese-only Nobunaga games before you get to the worldwide release of Pokemon Conquest.

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:27.380
What?

00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:34.140
Which is a crossover between the Pokemon franchise and Nobunaga's Ambition.

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:36.220
Yes. That's bullshit.

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:37.840
Yep. It's on DS.

00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:39.120
You can play it.

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:41.680
It's a real thing.

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:49.000
Jesus. And the only other real thing we have left to do, although it's hard to follow up that act,

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:55.780
is vote on whether Shinjin the Ruler, not Nobunaga, will be on the Essential Games list.

00:32:59.020 --> 00:33:04.040
Whether Shinjin was the guy who actually unified Japan or that was Nobunaga,

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:09.920
it's tough to say because Nobunaga got a lot more video games based off of him,

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:12.500
so that would kind of throw it in his category.

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:17.240
If I was just looking at the raw numbers of it all, I would say it's probably more of a Nobunaga thing.

00:33:18.340 --> 00:33:22.220
Shinjin the Ruler, as we talked about, did make combat a little more fun,

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:26.440
but it didn't really fix any of the other problems I have with strategy games.

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:32.940
So I'm just saying as, like, a 30-plus-year-old that, like, I'm still a little confused on what's happening,

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:36.620
and I really doubt anybody in 1990 was having a great time with it.

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:40.900
In fact, I would go as far as to say that even the videos, and they're few and far between,

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:46.700
but the videos you find of people playing these kinds of grand strategy NES games on YouTube

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:51.820
where, like, they do some kind of commentary, they also haven't quite figured it out.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:57.420
It's weird that they're like, here's me playing this game I had from my childhood for five hours,

00:33:57.440 --> 00:34:02.280
and as they talk through it, they're just kind of like, yeah, and I'm just going to do this,

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:08.440
and then, like, long pause, and then, okay, and yeah, we're going to add these guys to our military.

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:12.320
Like, they're not really sure what they're doing, so how can anybody be sure what they're doing?

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:18.900
I feel like to conquer Japan was probably a fluke back in 1540,

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:23.020
just as much as it would be a fluke to win a game of one of these.

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:24.600
So it's a no from me.

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:32.180
Sean? Yeah, I mean, in the same, like, also just sort of going back to seeing how other people have played these games.

00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:36.020
I remember, like, because there's no Wikipedia page for this game,

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:40.280
I just had to just sort of scour whatever Google had, just for a little bit of background.

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:42.380
I don't want to just go in completely blind.

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:49.700
Seeing the, there was like a Reddit post of like, ah, 32 years later I finally beat this game,

00:34:49.700 --> 00:34:56.400
and then everyone else just like, yeah, I couldn't, I, like, I wouldn't want to commit a whole year to playing this game and understand it,

00:34:56.500 --> 00:34:59.860
and like, oh, there's, you know, there's just too much going on, it's too difficult,

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:04.300
it's too, it's too just out there.

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:06.480
I kind of agree.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:10.000
I think this is, this whole genre just is not doing it for me,

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:14.220
and it's so weird because I like this stuff.

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:19.640
And I, I just wish that I had a, a better grasp of it.

00:35:19.840 --> 00:35:25.400
If I actually gave this a month of playtime, which I don't know if I can,

